THESE ARE THE THINGS THAT WE ARE TO CHANGE INN THE DATABASE

Invitation.token: the ERD names the primary key token. I named it tokenHash because storing raw tokens means a database leak hands out working invitation links. The raw token only ever appears in the emailed link, and we compare hashes. You’ll need to note this in the ERD and SRS.
MemberEvent is a free-text log. FR-015 asks for structured membership periods (start, end, unit, cause). That’s still the gap from my earlier list. We can add structure when we build the members module.
ImportReport is missing fields the SRS asks for (file digest, operator, per-category counts). I kept it to the ERD for now so it stays simple. We’ll extend it when we write the roster import.
Invitations have no “accepted at” field, so a used invitation can’t be told apart from an unused one. Decide with your team whether to add acceptedAt.


startsAt / endsAt replace activity_date + start_time + end_time. The SRS says “start and end datetime” and requires all times stored in UTC, and three separate fields invite bugs with midnight crossings and time zones. The date can always be derived from startsAt.
typeLabel is an SRS addition (FR-030).
The ERD says “responsible office” nowhere, but FR-030 requires it. I left a responsiblePositionId out so as not to add another relation. Decide whether to add it.
Attendance amendments (FR-033) must keep the original mark retrievable. AttendanceMark is updatable in this schema, so the original can only be found in ActivityEvent.text. That works for the MVP if we write structured text, but a dedicated amendment table would be cleaner. Raise it with the team.
AttendanceMark has no club_id. Isolation depends on the activity belonging to the club, so queries must always join through Activity. I’ll point this out again when we write the attendance service.


Make the minutes a constant, not a magic number. And add it to the SRS as a new criterion on FR-032.

FR-032: add a criterion that marking opens 20 minutes before the start and never for a cancelled activity.
FR-030: club-managed activity types; the responsible office is a position.
FR-033/034: the amendment definition, the rate formula, and the ActivityType and AttendanceAmendment tables.

The rate definition is a decision for your team, because FR-034 says “resulting attendance rate” without a formula. I’d propose present ÷ (present + absent), with excused activities excluded from both sides, and activities with no mark at all counted as a separate “unmarked” figure rather than silently as absences. Whatever you pick, put it in the SRS so tests can check it.

FR-032: add a criterion that marking opens 20 minutes before the start and never for a cancelled activity.
FR-030: club-managed activity types; the responsible office is a position.
FR-033/034: the amendment definition, the rate formula, and the ActivityType and AttendanceAmendment tables.



Design notes (and where I deviated from the ERD)
The composite foreign key on LedgerEntry.budgetLine. The ERD gives an entry both year_id and budget_line_id, which could disagree (an entry in 2026 pointing at a 2025 budget line). Referencing (budget_line_id, year_id, direction) makes the database reject that, and also rejects an expenditure posted against an income line. That’s why BudgetLine has the @@unique([id, yearId, direction]) line. It’s unusual-looking Prisma, but the SQL it produces is simple.
How reversals work. A reversing entry has the same direction and amount as the original and points at it through reverses_entry_id. An entry counts toward balances only if it is not a reversal and nothing reverses it, so both the original and its reversal drop out together. I chose same-direction because an opposite-direction reversal would break the composite key above. AC-041.2 says the balance covers “every non-reversed entry,” which is ambiguous about the reversing entry itself, so update the SRS wording to state this rule. The service must also enforce that the reversal matches the original’s direction and amount, and that a reversal can’t itself be reversed.
ERD says a reversal link is “0..N”, but the unique index limits each entry to one reversal. That matches the intent (correct once, then post a fresh entry), but confirm with your team.
posted_by and added_by reference Member, consistent with the other actor columns. The ERD didn’t mark them as foreign keys.
openingBalanceMinor is an addition. FR-045’s report starts from an opening balance, and nothing in the ERD stores one for the first year.
Money is Int. That holds up to about 2.1 billion minor units, roughly 21 million shillings if minor units are cents. Fine for a club. If your pilot disagrees, we’d switch to BigInt, which needs extra care when sending JSON.





